Virtual threads — Java twenty-one's big shift.

This is the headline change of modern Java. For twenty-five years, a Java thread was expensive, and every concurrency tool — pools, async callbacks, reactive frameworks — existed largely to ration threads. Virtual threads, finalized in Java twenty-one, make a thread so cheap you can have millions — and in doing so, they retire most of that complexity. It is the most consequential concurrency feature Java has ever added.

First, the old cost: platform threads are operating-system threads. A classic Java thread — now called a platform thread — is a thin wrapper over an operating-system thread. That's the source of the cost. Each operating-system thread reserves about a megabyte of stack, and is scheduled by the kernel, so a machine tops out at a few thousand of them. On a server that handles each request on its own thread, that ceiling is your maximum concurrency. And it's the reason thread pools exist — to share a scarce resource.

Now the idea. A virtual thread is a thread managed by the java virtual machine, not the operating system. Millions can exist, because they're just objects on the heap, not kernel resources. The J-V-M runs them on a small pool of platform threads — called carriers. And here's the trick: when a virtual thread hits a blocking call — input-output, a database, a sleep — the J-V-M unmounts it from its carrier and parks it, freeing that carrier to run another virtual thread. When the blocking call is ready, the virtual thread is remounted, and it continues. So a blocked virtual thread costs almost nothing — no operating-system thread is tied up waiting. Thousands can be blocked on input-output while a handful of carriers stay busy doing real work.

The payoff is the revolution: you write plain, blocking, sequential code — the easy kind — and it scales like complex asynchronous code. No callbacks, no reactive pipelines. Just read, then process, then write, top to bottom — one virtual thread per task. So: try, open-paren, var pool equals executors-dot-new-virtual-thread-per-task-executor, close-paren; then, for each request, pool-dot-submit of a lambda that handles it. And a blocking handle call is completely fine now. You can start one directly with thread-dot-of-virtual-dot-start, of a runnable. And new-virtual-thread-per-task-executor gives you one virtual thread per task — a model that was insane with platform threads, and is now the recommended default for server workloads.

Here's the rule that flips. Every instinct from the last section inverts. Platform threads are pooled, because they're scarce — you ration them. Virtual threads are not pooled, because they're abundant — you create one per task, and let it block freely. Pooling them would re-introduce the very scarcity they eliminate. So the old advice, "never block a thread", becomes "blocking a virtual thread is fine — that's the whole point". Reserve a bounded pool for genuinely C-P-U-bound work; use virtual threads for the far more common input-output-bound work.

Finally, where it fits — and its limits. Virtual threads shine for input-output-bound, high-concurrency work: web servers, A-P-I gateways, anything that spends its time waiting on the network or disk, with thousands of concurrent tasks. They do not speed up C-P-U-bound work — a virtual thread still needs a carrier, an operating-system thread on a core, to compute, so raw number-crunching is bounded by cores, as always. And two caveats to know: heavy use of synchronized blocks can pin a virtual thread to its carrier, defeating the unmount; and virtual threads don't reduce total compute — they remove the waiting tax. Understood that way, they're the feature that makes simple, readable, one-thread-per-task code the right default again — and the foundation for structured concurrency, two sections on.
